== 2. new edges s(n) and their runs ==
  s(n) = floor(sqrt(2n-1)) - floor(sqrt(n)): equals the brute-force count for n <= 5000 and the square count for 2 <= n <= 10^6
  runs (first, last, s) for n <= 60: [(1, 2, 0), (3, 3, 1), (4, 4, 0), (5, 12, 1), (13, 15, 2), (16, 18, 1), (19, 32, 2), (33, 35, 3), (36, 40, 2), (41, 48, 3), (49, 50, 2), (51, 60, 3)]
  s(n) - s(n-1) = [n = ceil((j^2+1)/2)] - [n = k^2] verified for n < 3000
  first gains (square j^2 enters at n = ceil((j^2+1)/2)): [3, 5, 9, 13, 19, 25, 33, 41, 51, 61]
  first losses (square k^2 leaves at n = k^2): [4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81]
  simultaneous gain and loss (invisible run boundaries) below 6400: [9, 25, 289, 841]
  all coincidences n <= 10^7: [1, 9, 25, 289, 841, 9801, 28561, 332929, 970225] = k^2 for k in [1, 3, 5, 17, 29, 99, 169, 577, 985] (OEIS A079496;
    odd-index Pell numbers 1, 5, 29, 169, 985 and even-index half-companion Pell numbers 3, 17, 99, 577;
    n = 1 satisfies the Pell condition but is degenerate: its window [2, 1] is empty)
  (at n = 25 the square 25 leaves the window [n+1, 2n-1] exactly as 49 enters: 7^2 - 2*5^2 = -1)

